ement  you know those people who have the power  to charge you with a crime so for  example the six pointed star that's  something that you usually associate  with Judaism however that is also the  symbol of a gang in Chicago so if  they're entering the six pointed star  into a gang database saying that people  who have this are more likely to be  members of the Chicago gang than people  who you know are expect you know  expressing you know  right in Judaism and get a six-pointed  star may find themselves marked that way  and beyond that some gang members leave  gangs and move on with their lives so  later on they may be the targets of  harassment by the cops over the  meaningless ink on their skin but aren't  tattoos a public statement to the world  something that says who we are without  actually having to say it you're walking  down the street you put your tattoo on  your arm it's a publicly available thing  you lose your right to privacy you walk  around in public I mean I would I  wouldn't  to an extent yes you you're out in  public you are showing things off but a  lot of the tattoos we saw our tattoos  under people's shirts under bunch of  people's chests on people's private  areas mas is talking about images used  in research by the National Institute of  Standards and Technology or NIST a  government institution who partners with  the FBI on biometrics easily and  accurately ascertaining the identity of  individuals is what biometric systems  are all about and the FBI worked with  NIST to compile a large database of some  15,000 plus images of tattoos to start  testing how various algorithms can match  tattoos but if you look closely at the  research you can see how law enforcement  could easily identify certain groups of  people and they're definitely looking  for religious or what they say is like  ritual realistic tattoos right so this  is interesting cuz this is something  that they've now redacted or retro  actively removed from a lot of their  material with the series of studies that  they started off with they had a few  goals one of the goals was to match  people who have similar tattoos in  practice the idea would be okay so this  is what a certain gangs tattoo looks  like can we match all of the gang  members who have the similar tattoo and  then we have this good network of gang  members but that's not what they were  using in the actual research in the  research they were frequently using  images like the cross Jesus on the  crucifix hands praying with a rosary  those were images they use time and time  again and in practice with this research  once you put one cross up there you're  pulling up everybody else who has a  cross  and you know ultimately creating a list  of people who have tattoos of a certain  religion miss denied this to us in an  email saying their research was not  about discerning meaning from tattoos or  categorizing people based on tattoos and  all that they were trying to do was help  ensure the technology was evaluated  using sound science that improved  accuracy but regardless of the intent  the ability to match or find similarity  in tattoos is the point of the research  and even though NIST is saying they  aren't discerning meaning here the cops  could definitely discern meaning after  all the FBI has a well publicized  history of profiling people with their  religion in mind to really understand  the concerns automated tattoo  recognition would mean for people you  have to imagine the big data world  policing it's moving towards most of  that is hard to see for sure but whether  it's drones with cameras license plate  readers that can location track or  closed-circuit cameras that can  recognize your face law enforcement  wants to know as much as they can about  citizens and tattoos are a part of that  I do see that there is a public safety  interest on law enforcement to track  gang members but the question is at what  point are we actually talking about a  gang or we're just talking about a club  of people or people who have a First  Amendment a right to associate you know  if you can identify that there is a  criminal enterprise involved alright I  can see that but when you're capturing  tattoos of every single person walking  in the door or you're starting to apply  tattoo recognition to CCTV or if you're  scraping tattoos off of Facebook and  using that to identify people just in  general I mean that's worrisome that's  how are we worried about with facial  recognition that it's not just about  tracking criminals but tracking everyone  tattoos could function in the same way  [Music]

